WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:08.960
- [Interviewer] All right, Kayla.

00:00:08.960 --> 00:00:11.960
Kayla, where'd you grow up,
where are you from originally?

00:00:11.960 --> 00:00:13.910
- I was born in Sevierville, Tennessee,

00:00:15.530 --> 00:00:18.193
but I've been in North
Carolina since I was five.

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.503
I'm 29, so.

00:00:21.691 --> 00:00:23.800
- [Interviewer] And you came
to West Virginia at what age?

00:00:23.800 --> 00:00:26.050
- Last year. Last August.
- Oh, so you're new here?

00:00:26.050 --> 00:00:27.620
- Yeah, yup.

00:00:27.620 --> 00:00:32.123
I came up here last August with my ex.

00:00:33.035 --> 00:00:34.540
- [Interviewer] So, a
man brought you here?

00:00:34.540 --> 00:00:35.963
- Yeah. Yup.

00:00:36.950 --> 00:00:40.410
- [Interviewer] How different
is the South Western part

00:00:40.410 --> 00:00:41.920
of West, Virginia to where you grew up

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:43.573
in Tennessee or North Carolina?

00:00:46.150 --> 00:00:49.360
- From Tennessee, I guess
it's a lot different

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:51.580
'cause it's a tourist community,
so during the summertime

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:53.230
it's packed full of people

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:56.890
and during the winter time it's empty.

00:00:56.890 --> 00:00:58.210
- [Interviewer] Just
very rural all the time?

00:00:58.210 --> 00:01:00.730
- Yeah, it's the same people all the time,

00:01:00.730 --> 00:01:04.030
it's the same places all the time, it's.

00:01:04.030 --> 00:01:08.633
Winston-Salem, it's so big
and there's so many people,

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:11.980
it's different people all the time.

00:01:11.980 --> 00:01:12.960
Yeah.

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:14.770
So, it's definitely different here

00:01:14.770 --> 00:01:16.310
compared to Winston or Tennessee,

00:01:16.310 --> 00:01:19.940
because it's always the
same thing all the time.

00:01:19.940 --> 00:01:20.773
- [Interviewer] And
how was your childhood?

00:01:20.773 --> 00:01:22.770
You grew up with both your parents?

00:01:22.770 --> 00:01:26.700
- No, my mom left my stepdad,
or my mom left my dad

00:01:26.700 --> 00:01:29.963
when I was 17 months old
and married his brother.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.620
So, from 17 months to when I was five,

00:01:34.620 --> 00:01:36.770
it was daddy-uncle (laughing).

00:01:36.770 --> 00:01:38.080
Uncle-daddy.

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:39.743
- [Interviewer] How would
you describe your childhood?

00:01:41.430 --> 00:01:43.830
- I don't really remember
a lot of my childhood.

00:01:47.360 --> 00:01:50.993
I was molested when I was six by my uncle,

00:01:53.260 --> 00:01:57.070
and doctor says that's
why I don't remember

00:01:57.070 --> 00:01:57.980
a lot of my childhood.
- Your uncle being

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.300
your mom's second husband?

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:01.750
- My mom's, no, my mom's brother.

00:02:01.750 --> 00:02:03.480
- [Interviewer] Okay, I'm
losing track of it (laughing).

00:02:03.480 --> 00:02:05.987
- I know, 'cause it
was, it was daddy.uncle,

00:02:05.987 --> 00:02:08.878
that kinda throws things off, but.

00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:10.410
- [Interviewer] That started at six,

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:11.900
how long did that last?

00:02:11.900 --> 00:02:15.473
- It just happened once
that I can recall anyways,

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:20.340
but no she didn't, she
still doesn't believe

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:21.860
that it happened.

00:02:21.860 --> 00:02:22.800
- [Interviewer] Your
mom didn't believe you?

00:02:22.800 --> 00:02:24.263
- No, she still doesn't.

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:28.160
She doesn't believe the
doctors or anything,

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.230
she believes what she wants to.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:33.720
She's an alcoholic, so I guess addiction

00:02:33.720 --> 00:02:35.030
kinda runs in our family.

00:02:35.030 --> 00:02:36.163
- [Interviewer] Yeah, that happens a lot.

00:02:36.163 --> 00:02:36.996
- Yeah.

00:02:39.090 --> 00:02:40.660
- [Interviewer] How far
did you go in school?

00:02:40.660 --> 00:02:41.493
- 10th grade.

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.847
Not my choice, my step, or my real dad.

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:53.573
I went to a high school in Pigeon Forge.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:03:00.700
He kinda lost his mind

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.563
a little bit over Christmas.

00:03:05.080 --> 00:03:07.760
He didn't have weed, he
is like Jekyll and Hyde

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:09.450
if he doesn't have his pot.

00:03:09.450 --> 00:03:13.580
And, he sent me away for
Christmas, and when I came back

00:03:13.580 --> 00:03:16.690
he just wasn't right,

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:19.450
and I stayed with my cousin for a few days

00:03:19.450 --> 00:03:22.130
and he called and told me
to come and get my stuff,

00:03:22.130 --> 00:03:23.940
and I'm scared to go to school

00:03:23.940 --> 00:03:26.520
after I came and went into
the house and got my things,

00:03:26.520 --> 00:03:30.420
and come to find out, and he
found out truancy was a thing,

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:33.290
and so he put a warrant
out for me being a runaway,

00:03:33.290 --> 00:03:37.430
I turned myself in and spent
a few days in juvenile hall.

00:03:37.430 --> 00:03:41.730
And they released me

00:03:41.730 --> 00:03:44.710
into his custody and he'd
beat the shit out of me,

00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:45.623
almost killed me,

00:03:46.620 --> 00:03:49.410
and I just never went
back to school after that.

00:03:49.410 --> 00:03:53.300
I want to, but life just hasn't

00:03:53.300 --> 00:03:57.230
really given me that opportunity yet, but.

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.810
- [Interviewer] Did you,
you went out on your own

00:03:58.810 --> 00:03:59.643
at what age?

00:04:01.040 --> 00:04:02.140
- When i quit school?

00:04:02.140 --> 00:04:03.393
- [Interviewer] Yeah, when
you went out on your own

00:04:03.393 --> 00:04:05.240
and lived without your parents.

00:04:06.680 --> 00:04:10.080
- I I've always kinda
lived it with my parents.

00:04:10.080 --> 00:04:12.300
I've always ended up going
back home to my mom's,

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.900
it's always been back and
forth, back and forth.

00:04:14.900 --> 00:04:18.900
I've been homeless, I was
homeless in new Orleans

00:04:18.900 --> 00:04:20.760
and I was homeless in
Michigan and I was homeless

00:04:20.760 --> 00:04:24.870
in Florida, but all of this has just been

00:04:24.870 --> 00:04:26.623
over the last five years.

00:04:26.623 --> 00:04:28.043
- [Interviewer] Is it because of drugs?

00:04:29.630 --> 00:04:33.103
- Drugs, it started because of depression,

00:04:34.690 --> 00:04:38.010
and a man, I got started on crack

00:04:38.010 --> 00:04:40.830
because of a previous relationship,

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.800
and I seek to my kid's dad for help,

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:47.620
and, you know, told him what was going on

00:04:47.620 --> 00:04:49.940
and asked him to keep the
kids for a little bit longer,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:53.600
and next thing I know he's telling me

00:04:53.600 --> 00:04:56.520
that he's not giving them
back, and I haven't seen

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:00.016
or spoke to them in almost five years.

00:05:00.016 --> 00:05:01.360
- [Interviewer] And
you have how many kids?

00:05:01.360 --> 00:05:03.230
- I have two, I have an
eight year old daughter

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:05.020
and a six year old son.

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:06.010
- [Interviewer] And they're staying with?

00:05:06.010 --> 00:05:07.380
- Their dad and his girlfriend.

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:10.930
Yeah, she's a big blessing.

00:05:10.930 --> 00:05:14.640
If it weren't for her, there's no telling

00:05:14.640 --> 00:05:15.940
where my kids would be at.

00:05:17.510 --> 00:05:21.090
So, she works two full-time jobs

00:05:21.090 --> 00:05:24.180
and make sure that they have everything

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.430
that they want and need, and they get to

00:05:26.430 --> 00:05:28.440
their doctor's appointment sitting.

00:05:28.440 --> 00:05:29.920
He's still a deadbeat, but

00:05:32.330 --> 00:05:36.313
at least he's got the babies, I guess.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:38.503
But.
- It's nice

00:05:38.503 --> 00:05:39.890
that they're taken care of by somebody.

00:05:39.890 --> 00:05:41.400
- Yeah.
- And that you like her.

00:05:41.400 --> 00:05:42.380
- Yeah.

00:05:42.380 --> 00:05:45.763
I didn't, as, I mean,

00:05:45.763 --> 00:05:47.250
I wouldn't say that I
like her, (laughing).

00:05:47.250 --> 00:05:50.253
- But you're.
- But I'm thankful for her.

00:05:51.403 --> 00:05:52.236
Sort of.

00:05:53.503 --> 00:05:55.813
- [Interviewer] And how do
you support yourself now?

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:02.840
- All kinds of different
ways, if I can find work

00:06:02.840 --> 00:06:04.490
then I'll do it.

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.640
I do everything from
painting, to concrete,

00:06:06.640 --> 00:06:07.733
to dry wall.

00:06:11.720 --> 00:06:14.313
Flooring, I can like flooring,

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:20.324
to having to turn tricks, yeah.

00:06:20.324 --> 00:06:21.750
- [Interviewer] A lot
of the girls do that.

00:06:21.750 --> 00:06:24.265
- Yeah. I mean, it's easy (giggling).

00:06:24.265 --> 00:06:25.650
- [Interviewer] Easy and fast money.

00:06:25.650 --> 00:06:28.723
- It's really degrading, and it really is,

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:35.953
it hits your heart and your self respect

00:06:35.953 --> 00:06:37.860
and, you know, your self worth,

00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:40.593
'cause it kinda makes you
feel a little bit worthless.

00:06:41.480 --> 00:06:43.200
But.
- Do you feel bad

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:45.360
about doing it afterwards?

00:06:45.360 --> 00:06:46.740
- Oh yeah. Yeah.

00:06:46.740 --> 00:06:48.490
Because I mean, that's
not who I am, that's not

00:06:48.490 --> 00:06:50.340
what I wanna do, you know?

00:06:50.340 --> 00:06:51.950
I wanna do those things with somebody

00:06:51.950 --> 00:06:53.770
that I actually care about, you know,

00:06:53.770 --> 00:06:57.130
but, I mean, I wanna eat too

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:58.969
and I wanna get high (laughing).

00:06:58.969 --> 00:07:00.430
- [Interviewer] What is your drug today?

00:07:00.430 --> 00:07:02.170
- Heroin. Yeah.
- Heroine?

00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:03.003
- [Interviewer] Yeah, so when you need it,

00:07:03.003 --> 00:07:04.230
you need it.
- Yeah.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:08.630
Like right now I'm a little
under the weather, you know?

00:07:08.630 --> 00:07:09.463
But,

00:07:12.570 --> 00:07:14.100
my day was just getting started,

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:15.270
like I had just woke up (laughing).

00:07:15.270 --> 00:07:17.113
- When I found you?
- Yeah.

00:07:18.380 --> 00:07:21.580
Yeah, I woke up and came
straight over that way.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.220
- [Interviewer] Do you have any friends?

00:07:23.220 --> 00:07:24.170
- I have a lot of friends here,

00:07:24.170 --> 00:07:25.793
I wouldn't call them friends.

00:07:27.550 --> 00:07:30.910
I have one good friend, he is actually

00:07:30.910 --> 00:07:33.513
who I've been staying
with, his name's Roy.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:36.313
He's pretty cool.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:39.520
I definitely wouldn't call them friends,

00:07:39.520 --> 00:07:41.420
'cause friends don't lie to one another,

00:07:41.420 --> 00:07:43.070
they don't steal from one another

00:07:44.461 --> 00:07:46.311
and everybody's steals from you here.

00:07:48.400 --> 00:07:50.353
But I know a lot of people,

00:07:51.252 --> 00:07:53.360
you know, so that's kinda helpful.

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:54.593
- [Interviewer] Giving you a new trust?

00:07:55.490 --> 00:07:59.103
- No. Not here or anywhere.

00:08:01.290 --> 00:08:03.380
Just God.

00:08:03.380 --> 00:08:08.380
I don't even trust myself
really, but I do trust God.

00:08:09.030 --> 00:08:10.553
So.
- Are you religious?

00:08:11.963 --> 00:08:14.690
- More so spiritual, I guess.

00:08:14.690 --> 00:08:16.723
I mean, I think every religions' right,

00:08:17.820 --> 00:08:19.570
'cause at the end of the
day, whether you believe

00:08:19.570 --> 00:08:22.300
in that door knob over there,
or you believe in yourself,

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:25.580
is through your God, you
still believe in something

00:08:25.580 --> 00:08:27.440
bigger than what you are.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.930
So, I don't know.

00:08:29.930 --> 00:08:31.840
I guess I would say I'm more so spiritual,

00:08:31.840 --> 00:08:36.253
I believe that, I like to
think there's a God, you know,

00:08:37.240 --> 00:08:41.650
that's why I'm still
here, but I shouldn't be.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.863
- Are you happy.
- No.

00:08:48.060 --> 00:08:49.523
- [Interviewer] You're
depressed sometimes?

00:08:57.030 --> 00:08:58.630
What's been the toughest
part of your life?

00:08:58.630 --> 00:08:59.480
Losing your kids?

00:09:00.450 --> 00:09:03.023
- Yeah. My family.

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:10.510
- [Interviewer] What's been
the best time of your life?

00:09:10.510 --> 00:09:12.793
- Hmm. Being a mom.

00:09:17.600 --> 00:09:20.000
- [Interviewer] Do you ever
consider leaving this area?

00:09:20.000 --> 00:09:21.890
- Oh God, every day.

00:09:21.890 --> 00:09:23.060
Every day.
- It's a tough region,

00:09:23.060 --> 00:09:24.607
I mean, it seems like
everyone is into drugs

00:09:24.607 --> 00:09:26.980
and there's all kinds of other
crazy things going on here.

00:09:26.980 --> 00:09:27.813
- Yeah.

00:09:27.813 --> 00:09:30.393
I mean, if I go back home,
I'm still gonna be using,

00:09:31.660 --> 00:09:32.493
but,

00:09:35.190 --> 00:09:40.150
I'll be around people that
act like they care more,

00:09:40.150 --> 00:09:42.023
at least, even if they don't,

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:49.890
they at least act like they do, you know.

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.080
I've got more places that I can stay,

00:09:52.080 --> 00:09:54.100
so I don't have to worry about being cold

00:09:54.100 --> 00:09:56.190
during the winter time.

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:59.570
But, it's closer to my kids

00:09:59.570 --> 00:10:02.140
that I know I can't be near,

00:10:02.140 --> 00:10:04.453
so it kinda helps being further away.

00:10:05.920 --> 00:10:07.350
- [Interviewer] This is
a very difficult part

00:10:07.350 --> 00:10:10.530
of the country it seems, that
so many people are into drugs.

00:10:10.530 --> 00:10:12.880
- [Kayla] Oh yeah, I mean it's,
it's, it's really bad here,

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:15.680
that's all I hear about all
the time is so-and-so died,

00:10:15.680 --> 00:10:19.290
so-and-so OD'd and you
know, there was a guy here

00:10:19.290 --> 00:10:23.470
named Too Tall, and he was,

00:10:23.470 --> 00:10:26.960
they went up into his house
and they cut him ear to ear

00:10:26.960 --> 00:10:28.700
while he sat in his recliner,

00:10:28.700 --> 00:10:30.960
and then everybody just
ran up into his house

00:10:30.960 --> 00:10:32.500
while there was still crime scene tape up

00:10:32.500 --> 00:10:35.033
and stole all of his
stuff out of his house.

00:10:35.890 --> 00:10:37.420
Like, people are so heartless here

00:10:37.420 --> 00:10:40.180
because all they care
about is getting high,

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:43.260
and how they're gonna get that next buzz

00:10:43.260 --> 00:10:46.400
or just even really now, you're
not even getting high now,

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:47.650
you're just getting well.

00:10:49.150 --> 00:10:49.983
But.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:52.680
- [Interviewer] It seems
like there's so many,

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:54.720
it looks like such a beautiful

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:57.510
and serene part of the country,
and yet you start hearing

00:10:57.510 --> 00:10:59.012
all these stories of

00:10:59.012 --> 00:11:00.720
crazy human behavior.
- Yeah.

00:11:00.720 --> 00:11:05.720
It's, oh well I don't know what
the word would be for that.

00:11:06.170 --> 00:11:08.145
It's fucked up.

00:11:08.145 --> 00:11:09.740
- [Interviewer] That's the
word for it (laughing).

00:11:09.740 --> 00:11:10.978
That's the word.

00:11:10.978 --> 00:11:12.860
- [Kayla] It's such a beautiful place,

00:11:12.860 --> 00:11:14.390
that's what makes me wanna stay here,

00:11:14.390 --> 00:11:16.690
but the people are just so ugly.

00:11:16.690 --> 00:11:18.750
It's unreal, you know.

00:11:18.750 --> 00:11:20.640
- [Interviewer] In some parts
of the state, maybe here,

00:11:20.640 --> 00:11:22.750
maybe not, there's sex
trafficking going on?

00:11:22.750 --> 00:11:23.900
- [Kayla] Yeah. Yeah.

00:11:23.900 --> 00:11:25.660
I mean, it's real, I didn't believe it

00:11:25.660 --> 00:11:28.980
until I met some people, I mean,

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:33.040
they kidnap kids, they
kidnap women, you know?

00:11:33.040 --> 00:11:36.350
I mean, every time I go out,
whether it's day or night,

00:11:36.350 --> 00:11:38.250
you know, I have to fear,

00:11:38.250 --> 00:11:40.240
especially if I get
into somebody else's car

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:42.890
I have to worry about whether or not

00:11:42.890 --> 00:11:46.750
I'm gonna be shipped off to another state

00:11:46.750 --> 00:11:48.503
or across the border.

00:11:49.810 --> 00:11:53.180
You know, I've met three people
here just in the last year

00:11:53.180 --> 00:11:54.380
that had been kidnapped.

00:11:57.060 --> 00:12:00.440
I don't really know what
happened to them or anything,

00:12:00.440 --> 00:12:02.190
but I know that they were kidnapped

00:12:02.190 --> 00:12:06.260
and they were held hostage
in a place, you know?

00:12:06.260 --> 00:12:08.990
So that's scary, you don't
think that those kinds of things

00:12:08.990 --> 00:12:12.330
happen right here, you
know, where you live at.

00:12:12.330 --> 00:12:14.530
That's something that happens in Mexico

00:12:14.530 --> 00:12:18.110
or California maybe, you know,

00:12:18.110 --> 00:12:20.530
but not in West Virginia.

00:12:20.530 --> 00:12:23.129
- [Interviewer] Here
it's so, everyone is so,

00:12:23.129 --> 00:12:24.760
everything is so remote
that it would be easy

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:26.470
to get away with.
- Yeah.

00:12:26.470 --> 00:12:27.303
- [Interviewer] You want to
kill somebody and throw them

00:12:27.303 --> 00:12:28.795
in a ditch, no one's ever

00:12:28.795 --> 00:12:29.628
gonna find out.
- Oh yeah.

00:12:29.628 --> 00:12:33.870
You go up to Irish mountain,
nobody would ever know.

00:12:33.870 --> 00:12:36.180
I mean, there's so many
places in the woods here,

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:39.850
like there's places that
I've gone and and walked into

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:41.707
and I'm like, "Damn, this
would be a killer place

00:12:41.707 --> 00:12:43.170
"to hide a body," you know.

00:12:43.170 --> 00:12:44.003
- [Interviewer] The coyotes
and the bears would find it

00:12:44.003 --> 00:12:45.860
before anybody.
- Oh yeah.

